## Lore

The Gridgitz look like big, broad-shouldered, hairy Russians, and after
generations in Russia most of them think of themselves as exactly that. Many
did not learn they were D-Bees until the Sovietski began genetic screening,
and most shrugged and went back to the fields. They speak Russian, keep the
peasant traditions and are counted among the human population.

They are farmers, miners and laborers above all, proud of calloused hands
and long days, and they celebrate as hard as they work. Stubborn to a fault,
a Gridgitz who sets out to learn a trade can become a fine doctor, engineer,
mechanic or lawman, though few take that road.

Magic they will not touch, calling it "dishonest work". Yet about half of
them carry a psychic gift for healing, which many credit to prayer; the
strongest healers become village wise folk or Body Fixers, and the state''s
propaganda holds the Gridgitz up as the ideal Soviet peasant.

## GM Notes

Pronounced GRID-jitz. About 4% of the Sovietski population, counted in the
human figures. Height 6 feet to 6 feet 9 inches (1.8-2 m), 200 to 300 lbs (90
to 135 kg). Males live 2D6+66 years, females 4D6+70.

Disposition: hardworking, honest and polite to strangers, but they keep their
troubles to themselves and open up to almost no one. Warriors and adventurers
are respected less than farmers and laborers.

Habitat: rural Russia above all; every Russian village and city has some
(1D4% of its people). Slave market value about a human''s, double for a
trained healer, but they resist captivity and are seldom wanted.

Allies: whichever Russian power governs them. Woodland spirits are avoided
but thought well of. Enemies: Demons, Witches and Necromancers most of
all, then any practitioner of magic, gypsies and non-Russians generally.
', 'published', 'data-script'
